WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
32 major dams on the Tigris and Euphrates reduced the flood pulses that fed Iraq's Mesopotamian

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:13.534
marshes. This is unintended consequences, episode 145. In the early 2000s, Landsat images

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:19.474
of southern Iraq showed what those missing pulses had left behind. Vast read beds and shallow

00:00:19.474 --> 00:00:25.214
lakes had become cracked salt flats, dotted with dead fish and abandoned buffalo. The change

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:30.834
had not arrived in a single season. It accumulated as each new reservoir filled and held back the

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:36.794
high water peaks that once spread across the flood plain. Turkish, Syrian, and Iraqi engineers had

00:00:36.794 --> 00:00:42.134
built those dams to store water for hydropower and for year-round irrigation in upstream regions

00:00:42.134 --> 00:00:48.374
that had long suffered from unreliable rainfall. What the structures removed instead was the annual spring

00:00:48.374 --> 00:00:53.834
flood that had kept the downstream marshes alive from millennia. Without those pulses, the balance

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:58.834
between inflow and evaporation that had sustained the marshes for thousands of years tip toward

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:04.607
steady decline. To understand why the dams looked like the right answer, you have to stand where

00:01:04.607 --> 00:01:10.707
the planners stood, after the Second World War looking at dry land and an erratic river. Turkish

00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:16.227
planners launched the southeastern Anatolia project, known as Gap, to lift one of the country's

00:01:16.227 --> 00:01:22.367
poorest regions out of poverty. Their calculation was straightforward. Store winter and spring runoff

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:27.427
behind large dams, used that stored water to generate electricity for factories, and

00:01:27.427 --> 00:01:31.807
to water cotton, wheat, and fruit on land that had been too dry to farm reliably.

00:01:32.627 --> 00:01:35.867
Similar projects in Syria and Iraq followed the same logic.

00:01:36.647 --> 00:01:41.467
Rivers that had flooded unpredictably could be turned into reliable assets for national development.

00:01:42.107 --> 00:01:46.807
At the time, the marshes downstream appeared as remote wetlands whose seasonal rhythms would

00:01:46.807 --> 00:01:48.967
simply adjust to steadier, lower flows.

00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:55.087
The engineers worked with the data they possessed, river gauges, reservoir models, national

00:01:55.087 --> 00:01:55.947
growth targets.

00:01:56.487 --> 00:02:00.907
What those tools did not yet hold was a full picture of how tightly the marshes depended

00:02:00.907 --> 00:02:02.267
on the pulse of high water.

00:02:02.907 --> 00:02:07.527
Their models treated the river system as a linear supply chain rather than a pulsing delta,

00:02:07.987 --> 00:02:11.127
where the timing of delivery mattered as much as the total volume.

00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:14.707
That framing was rational given the questions they were asking.

00:02:15.347 --> 00:02:16.627
How much water can we hold?

00:02:16.627 --> 00:02:22.767
how much power can we make? How many hectares can we irrigate in a dry year? The marshes were

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:28.567
not inside that question. A wetland that flooded and receded on its own schedule did not look from

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:34.447
an upstream planning office like a customer with a delivery date. Construction accelerated through

00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:43.867
the 1970s and the 1980s. Turkey completed the Kebon Dam in 1974 and the Ataturk Dam in 1992. Syria

00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:49.827
Syria added the Tab Qadam, Iraq built its own storage reservoirs. By the early 1990s,

00:02:49.987 --> 00:02:55.247
more than 30 large dams stood along the two rivers. Their combined storage capacity exceeded

00:02:55.247 --> 00:03:00.607
three times the average annual flow, according to the 2001 report from the United Nations

00:03:00.607 --> 00:03:07.327
Environment Program. Early results looked promising. Turkish hydropower output rose sharply. New

00:03:07.327 --> 00:03:12.829
irrigation districts opened in the Haran plain. From the standpoint of the region's gap was

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:16.469
built to serve, the project was doing what it had been designed to do.

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:21.609
Water that used to rush past in a few violent weeks could now be metered out across a growing

00:03:21.609 --> 00:03:22.069
season.

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:26.429
Factories had power, fields that had waited on rain had a schedule.

00:03:27.149 --> 00:03:30.149
Downstream, the same success registered as a flattening.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:35.149
The spring flood peaks that had once reached the marshes began to drop in height and to arrive

00:03:35.149 --> 00:03:35.969
less often.

00:03:36.985 --> 00:03:40.565
Each new dam upstream altered the hydrograph in measurable ways.

00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:47.245
peak discharges fell. Base flows became more constant. The variability the marshes had evolved to

00:03:47.245 --> 00:03:52.725
exploit was the very thing the operating rules were written to remove. Iraqi authorities facing

00:03:52.725 --> 00:03:58.965
their own water shortages after the 1991 Gulf War started additional drainage works that accelerated

00:03:58.965 --> 00:04:05.718
the change. Those works were a response to scarcity, not a celebration of it. Less water was arriving.

00:04:05.718 --> 00:04:10.978
The remaining channels were cut, banked, and redirected toward uses the state could measure.

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:14.278
Upstream and downstream, the same idea was at work.

00:04:14.738 --> 00:04:17.498
Hold what you can, send onward what you do not need.

00:04:17.998 --> 00:04:22.798
Nobody in that chain was required to recreate a historical flood hundreds of kilometers away.

00:04:23.358 --> 00:04:27.698
The marshes had once covered between 15,000 and 20,000 square kilometers.

00:04:28.278 --> 00:04:29.458
They shrank dramatically.

00:04:29.998 --> 00:04:33.558
The 2001 assessment from the United Nations Environment Program,

00:04:33.558 --> 00:04:38.858
time, drawing on Landsat imagery, documented an 85% loss by 2000.

00:04:39.418 --> 00:04:46.018
The bulk of the rapid desiccation occurred between 1991 and 1995, when Iraqi drainage canals

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:48.598
and embankments severed remaining water sources.

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:54.178
The upstream dams had already cut the frequency and height of flood pulses over preceding decades,

00:04:54.718 --> 00:04:58.038
so less water reached the marshes even before that final drainage.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:04:59.798
The two losses stacked.

00:04:59.798 --> 00:05:04.498
In fact, first the pulse was shortened and lowered year by year as reservoirs filled.

00:05:05.018 --> 00:05:09.898
Then the last connections were cut on purpose in a few hard seasons because what remained

00:05:09.898 --> 00:05:13.018
no longer looked like a living floodplain worth leaving open.

00:05:13.718 --> 00:05:16.098
Without periodic flushing, salinity climbed.

00:05:16.778 --> 00:05:21.578
Reed beds died, the fishery that supported thousands of marsh Arab families collapsed.

00:05:22.038 --> 00:05:27.118
Those families had lived by fishing, by hurting buffalo, and by harvesting reeds for generations.

00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:33.098
A reed bed is not only habitat, it is building material, fodder, and a way of moving through

00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:33.858
shallow water.

00:05:34.378 --> 00:05:39.038
When the reeds died and the buffalo were abandoned on drying ground, the livelihood and

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:40.458
the landscape failed together.

00:05:41.178 --> 00:05:43.818
Dust storms increased as exposed lake beds dried.

00:05:44.398 --> 00:05:48.898
What had been a shallow sea of green became a source of windblown salt and silt for people

00:05:48.898 --> 00:05:50.738
who had never set a net in those waters.

00:05:50.738 --> 00:05:57.938
A paper published in 2025 in the journal MDPI Plants on Threatened Aquatic Species records

00:05:57.938 --> 00:06:04.858
vegetation losses between 80 and 99% across the major marsh units from 1982 to 2017.

00:06:05.408 --> 00:06:10.658
The paper attributes that long-term stress to reduced inflows from the dammed rivers alongside

00:06:10.658 --> 00:06:12.798
the drainage of the 1990s.

00:06:13.138 --> 00:06:15.378
The electricity and the crops went somewhere else.

00:06:15.738 --> 00:06:18.518
They benefited distant urban and agricultural centers.

00:06:18.518 --> 00:06:21.038
the places the dams had been built to serve.

00:06:21.518 --> 00:06:23.638
The causal chain ran through storage rules.

00:06:24.178 --> 00:06:27.158
Those rules maximized retention for power and irrigation.

00:06:27.878 --> 00:06:32.118
They carried no requirement to release water at the precise volume and timing needed

00:06:32.118 --> 00:06:35.678
to recreate historical flood peaks hundreds of kilometers downstream.

00:06:36.478 --> 00:06:41.598
Volume averaged over a year can look adequate on a ledger while the week that matters never

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:42.018
comes.

00:06:42.618 --> 00:06:44.658
A marsh does not drink an annual average.

00:06:44.658 --> 00:06:50.618
It drinks a pulse, a rise high enough to push fresh water across a flat, to flush salt,

00:06:51.078 --> 00:06:54.918
to wet the roots of reeds, and then a fall that leaves the shallows productive.

00:06:55.518 --> 00:06:59.738
Steady base flow, even when the yearly total is large, does not do that work.

00:07:00.398 --> 00:07:02.458
Evaporation in southern Iraq is relentless.

00:07:03.178 --> 00:07:08.038
Once the spring sheet of water stopped arriving, the balance tipped, and each dry year made

00:07:08.038 --> 00:07:09.478
the next one harder to reverse.

00:07:10.158 --> 00:07:12.938
That is a second order effect of a first order success.

00:07:12.938 --> 00:07:19.659
USS. The dams did store water. The turbines did turn. The Haran plain did green. The

00:07:19.659 --> 00:07:25.039
third order effect was social and atmospheric at once. Communities whose entire economy

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:29.919
was time to the flood lost the calendar they lived by, while dust from the emptied beds

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:35.699
traveled beyond the marsh edge. After 2003, some embankments were breached and partial

00:07:35.699 --> 00:07:40.999
refletting occurred. Reporting in wired, drawing on data from the United Nations Environment

00:07:40.999 --> 00:07:47.999
program, and from NASA, put the restoration at roughly 58% of the marshes by 2006. Water

00:07:47.999 --> 00:07:52.899
could be let back onto ground that drainage works had sealed. Recovery remained limited

00:07:52.899 --> 00:07:58.999
by continued upstream withdrawals, by higher salinity, and by reduced overall flow. Breaching

00:07:58.999 --> 00:08:03.639
a bank returns a path, it does not return a river that is being held upstream for someone

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:10.019
else's season. Turkey continued operating the gap reservoirs for power and irrigation. Downstream

00:08:10.019 --> 00:08:15.979
provinces faced recurring drought. No single country reversed its dam operations. Talks on

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:21.979
minimum flow agreements began and produced modest results. A minimum flow is a floor. It keeps

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:27.159
a channel from going dry. It is not the same instrument as a spring peak time to spread across

00:08:27.159 --> 00:08:32.959
a floodplain. The marshes that remain are smaller, more saline, and still vulnerable to any further

00:08:32.959 --> 00:08:38.499
reduction in spring runoff. Attempts to negotiate coordinated releases have repeatedly stalled.

00:08:38.499 --> 00:08:46.119
Each nation's operating rules still prioritize its own storage targets over the downstream pulse that once connected the entire basin.

00:08:46.739 --> 00:08:50.059
That stall is not mysterious once you look at what the rules reward.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:58.820
A reservoir operator who releases a flood peak on a downstream wetland schedule is giving away stored energy and stored irrigation days.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:04.640
The benefit lands in another country in a landscape that does not appear on the operator's production sheet.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.040
The cost lands at home in a misgeneration target or a shortfall in a cotton district.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.160
Under those incentives, modest minimum flow talks are the agreement that can be signed.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.960
A pulse large enough to matter is the agreement that keeps failing.

00:09:19.971 --> 00:09:25.311
When Turkish, Syrian, and Iraqi planners sized reservoirs to capture three times the river's

00:09:25.311 --> 00:09:29.951
average volume, they rewarded steady extraction for upstream farms and turbines.

00:09:29.951 --> 00:09:34.551
lines. They did not write a corresponding rule that protected the downstream flood pulse

00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:39.551
the marshes required. Later restoration showed the limit of a downstream only fix.

00:09:40.191 --> 00:09:44.551
Breaching drainage works could bring water back only if upstream releases still delivered

00:09:44.551 --> 00:09:50.231
enough seasonal volume. The path and the pulse are different requirements. Restoring one without

00:09:50.231 --> 00:09:55.031
the other leaves a smaller, saltier wetland that can be lost again in a dry run of years.

00:09:55.631 --> 00:10:00.751
Decision makers today, sizing new storage on transboundary rivers, can test whether

00:10:00.751 --> 00:10:05.311
their operating rules preserve at least the historical peak flow events that sustain wetlands

00:10:05.311 --> 00:10:07.071
hundreds of kilometers downstream.

00:10:07.671 --> 00:10:09.091
That test is specific.

00:10:09.631 --> 00:10:14.411
It asks not only how much water the reservoir will hold, but which week the water must still

00:10:14.411 --> 00:10:17.971
be allowed to rise, and who is accountable if that week never comes.

00:10:18.571 --> 00:10:23.151
If you follow environmental systems the way we do on environmental intelligence, the

00:10:23.151 --> 00:10:27.751
The Marsh story is a reminder that a river's average can look healthy while the event the

00:10:27.751 --> 00:10:30.591
wetland actually lives on has already been engineered out.

00:10:31.151 --> 00:10:35.751
How might current basin models change if they treated the Marsh flood pulse as a measurable

00:10:35.751 --> 00:10:38.531
delivery target rather than leftover water?

